@page "/exgrid/columns"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid

<DocsPage Product="ExGrid" Title="Columns"
          Lead="A column says how to read a value from a row, what type the value is, and how wide the column wants to be. Everything else — the format, the alignment, the tone — is optional, and the defaults follow the type.">

    <DocsSection Title="Declaring columns">
        <p>
            A <code>GridColumn&lt;TRow&gt;</code> takes a name, a <code>ColumnType</code> and a value accessor.
            The name addresses the column wherever the grid reports something about it — a sort, a filter,
            an edit — so it must be unique. The header defaults to the name.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>The type decides the behaviour.</strong> <code>Text</code>, <code>Number</code>,
                <code>Date</code> and <code>Boolean</code> each bring their own filter operators, comparison and
                alignment: numbers and dates align right, text and booleans left. A <code>Date</code> column
                says which date type its values are with <code>dateType</code> (<code>DateTime</code> unless declared).
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Widths are Auto or Fixed.</strong> An Auto column grows to fit what it has painted and never
                narrows on its own. <code>ColumnWidthSpec</code> also carries a minimum (40&nbsp;px by default)
                and a maximum for what the grid computes (400&nbsp;px). A Fixed width below the minimum is refused
                when the column is built, not clamped.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>format</code> is the one text a value shows.</strong> It is what the cell paints, what
                copy puts on the clipboard as text, what the filter's value list offers and what the editor opens with.
                Your delegate owns the culture; the grid takes no view on separators. Without one, a <code>double</code>
                shows at most 15 significant digits, as Excel's General does, so <code>0.1 + 0.2</code> shows
                <code>0.3</code>, as sorting and filtering already compared it. The value itself is not rounded: the copy a
                spreadsheet reads back carries it whole, and the editor opens on it whole, so an edit committed
                unchanged writes back the same number. A date shows one ISO form by its type.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>align</code> and <code>headerAlign</code></strong> override the type's alignment.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>tone</code> says what a value means</strong> — <code>CellTone.Positive</code> or
                <code>Negative</code> — and the theme says what colour that is. The bare grid paints no colour for a
                tone until you set <code>--ex-tone-positive-color</code> and <code>--ex-tone-negative-color</code>;
                ExGrid.MudBlazor maps them onto the palette's success and error colours.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            A number or a date too wide for its column is painted <code>####</code>, as Excel does, rather than cut
            short: a truncated amount looks like a smaller one. Widen the <em>Market value</em> column below and the
            values come back.
        </p>
        <p>
            One that fits is painted whole. Column edges are drawn on the screen's own pixels, so a column can come
            out up to one of them narrower than its width; a cell that shows a number or a date may use that pixel
            of its right padding. Text is not given it, so figures stand a pixel nearer the column's right edge than
            text aligned right.
        </p>
        <p>
            Whether a number or a date fits is judged from the widths its font paints, measured for
            <code>system-ui</code> on macOS, on Linux, and on Windows under an English or a Japanese display language,
            and for Roboto under the MudBlazor Wrappers. Under another Windows display language <code>system-ui</code>
            is another face, and a character it paints wider than measured is judged at the measured width, so such a
            value can come out cut at its column's edge rather than as <code>####</code>. It is one of 0.1.0's Known
            limitations.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Types, widths, formats and tones" Builtin="typeof(ColumnTypes)" Mud="typeof(ColumnTypesMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/Position.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Keep the column list stable">
        <p>
            Build the list once and keep it in a field. The list instance is the grid's signal that the columns
            changed: a new array on every render makes every row render again. When something does change — a
            width, the order — hand over a <em>new</em> list rather than editing the old one. Columns are immutable
            records for the same reason.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Pinned columns and Header Groups">
        <p>
            <code>PinnedColumnCount</code> holds the first <em>n</em> columns against the left edge while the rest
            scroll, like Excel's frozen panes. Pinned cells are painted at every scroll position, so pin a handful,
            not half the grid.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>HeaderGroups</code> draws labelled rectangles above the header row. A <code>HeaderGroup</code>
            names its member columns, which must stand next to each other; <code>tier</code> stacks groups above
            groups. The grid refuses, naming the group, a member that does not exist, members that are not adjacent,
            rectangles that overlap, and a group that straddles the pinned boundary.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Groups that open and close">
        <p>
            A group given an <code>id</code> and <code>expanded</code> draws a toggle at its left, a chevron
            pointing down while it is open and right while it is closed. A press on it, or
            <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>↑</kbd> from a cell under the group (<kbd>Option</kbd> on a Mac),
            raises <code>onHeaderGroupToggled</code> (<code>OnHeaderGroupToggled</code> in Blazor) with the group's
            <code>id</code> and the state asked for; <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>↓</kbd> opens the lowest
            closed group over the cell. The grid adds and removes no column: answer by handing back the columns you
            want and the group's new <code>expanded</code>, as you answer a hide or a reorder. Whether a group is
            open is the user's View State, yours to keep. A press anywhere else on the group is still its drag.
        </p>
        <p>
            The notification asks for the opposite of what the group showed when the input was taken, so two quick
            presses before your answer arrives ask for the same state twice, and applying both lands where one
            would. The Selection is dropped when the columns change, but the Focus survives the toggle: it comes
            back on the same row, in its own column when that is still shown and otherwise in the group's first
            member. A closed group keeps at least one column shown, a summary column say, so its toggle has
            somewhere to stand.
        </p>
        <p>
            A closed group can wear a <strong>Hidden Mark</strong>: give it <code>hiddenMark</code> with a Cell
            State and a reason, and it carries that state's corner mark, the reason as its tooltip and its
            accessible description. Use it when a hidden column holds something the reader must not lose sight of,
            such as a value the user set. The grid refuses, naming the group, <code>expanded</code> without an
            <code>id</code>, two groups with one <code>id</code>, <code>expanded</code> on a grid with no handler or
            on a spacer, and a Hidden Mark on a group that is not closed or whose reason is empty. Given the
            handler, <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>↓</kbd> and <kbd>↑</kbd> are the grid's and cannot be
            declared keys; without it they stay the page's.
        </p>
        <p>
            The toggle's picture and the mark's colour are the stylesheet's: <code>--ex-header-group-collapsed-icon</code>
            and <code>--ex-header-group-expanded-icon</code> are CSS images painted in
            <code>--ex-header-group-toggle-color</code>, and the mark takes its state's own token. The Mantine and
            MUI Wrappers, and ExGrid.MudBlazor, set them from their design system's chevrons and theme. In Blazor the
            toggle is drawn by the grid on the TypeScript core, <code>ExGrid.Script.ExGrid</code>; the C# core's grid
            refuses a group that declares one rather than draw it without.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="A group that opens and closes, with a Hidden Mark" Builtin="typeof(ExpandableGroups)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/Position.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Resizing, reordering and hiding">
        <p>
            Column layout is your application's state, and the grid only reports the gestures:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>OnColumnWidthChanged</code> fires when a resize drag is released. The grips appear only when it is wired.</li>
            <li><code>OnColumnOrderChanged</code> hands over the whole new order after a header drag. A drag never splits a group or crosses the pinned boundary.</li>
            <li><code>OnColumnHidden</code> and <code>OnPinnedCountChanged</code> back the column menu's <em>Hide</em> and <em>Pin</em> commands. <em>Hide</em> is offered only when <code>OnColumnHidden</code> is wired.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Answer each by pushing back a new <code>Columns</code> list. Drag a column edge, drag a header, or hide
            a column from its ▾ menu. In ExGrid for React the three are <code>onColumnWidthChanged</code>,
            <code>onColumnOrderChanged</code> and <code>onColumnHidden</code>, answered by handing back a new
            <code>columns</code> array.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Pinned columns, two tiers of groups, and layout events" Builtin="typeof(ColumnLayout)" Mud="typeof(ColumnLayoutMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/Position.cs", "Examples/Grid/ColumnOrder.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Keeping a view">
        <p>
            The columns a user shows, their order, their widths, how many are pinned, the sort and the filter are
            the user's <strong>View State</strong>, which your application keeps for them. The grid holds none of it,
            so a view is what you hand the grid. <code>GridViewState.Of</code> takes it from there — the
            <code>Columns</code> list, <code>PinnedColumnCount</code>, and the sort and the filter in force, a
            <code>Source</code>'s <code>Sorts</code> and <code>Filter</code> when you have one — and
            <code>GridViewStateJson.Write</code> turns it into JSON to store. <code>GridViewStateJson.Read</code>
            reads it back, told the names of every column you declare. Apply it as you answer the gestures: build the
            <code>Columns</code> list in its order at its widths, set <code>PinnedColumnCount</code>, and hand its
            sort and filter on.
        </p>
        <p>
            Filters and widths round-trip. Each filter operand keeps its type: a <code>decimal</code> is written as
            its exact digits, a <code>DateTime</code> with its kind, a <code>DateTimeOffset</code> with its offset,
            so a restored condition means what it meant. A width is <code>"Auto"</code> or its pixels, never the
            width an Auto column was painted at, so a restored Auto column measures itself again. A column the user
            hid is one the view leaves out. A <code>GridViewState</code> is one value: two compare equal when they
            hold the same view, so a view read back equals the one written, and comparing the stored view with
            <code>GridViewState.Of</code> tells you whether the user has changed it.
        </p>
        <p>
            A stored view that cannot be read is refused with a <code>JsonException</code> that names what is wrong
            — a column you no longer declare, a width that is neither form, an operand that is not its type (a
            <code>decimal</code> with more digits than a <code>decimal</code> holds included, which is refused rather
            than rounded), a version this release does not read — rather than restored in part. The JSON carries a version from its
            first release. <code>GridViewStateJson.WriteQuery</code> and <code>ReadQuery</code> do the same for a
            <code>GridQuery</code> alone, for your own server. An Opaque Filter has no JSON form here: its meaning
            is yours, so keep it beside the view. Nor does a view have any other: a <code>GridViewState</code>, a
            <code>ColumnViewState</code> or a <code>ColumnWidth</code> handed to <code>System.Text.Json</code>
            directly is refused with a <code>NotSupportedException</code> that names
            <code>GridViewStateJson</code>, since written that way a width and an operand would not come back as
            they went. Put the string <code>Write</code> returns into your own document instead.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Save and restore a view" Builtin="typeof(SavedView)" Mud="typeof(SavedViewMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/Position.cs"])">
            Widen a column, drag a header, pin from a column's ▾ menu, sort, and filter <em>P&amp;L</em> to values
            above 0. Save the view, press Reset, and restore it: the stored JSON below the grid is what comes back.
            Save or restore again without changing anything, and the Example says so: it compares the view read
            back with the one on screen.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Mark, Template and Action columns">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong><code>GridColumn.MarkColumn()</code></strong> puts a checkbox on every row and one in the header
                that marks the whole result. The marks belong to the rows, not to the data:
                <code>GridSource.From</code> keeps them for you in <code>Marks</code>, by row instance, across sorting,
                filtering and edits.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Under live data</strong>, a press on a mark is judged against what was on screen when it was
                made, which over a slow connection may have changed by the time it arrives. What the press can still
                name exactly is honoured: a row's checkbox marks the row it was pressed on, wherever the order has
                moved it, and the header's checkbox marks what it named when it was pressed — under a pager, the page
                it was pressed on, even when the page has been turned or the pager taken away since; with no pager, the
                whole result, even when a pager has been added since. The rest marks nothing, so no mark lands on a
                row nobody pressed, and
                <code>OnMarkRefused</code> says why, once per press: <code>OrderMoved</code> when the header's checkbox
                or "Mark all N rows" was pressed before a sort, a filter or a live reorder moved the rows it named,
                and <code>SourceChanged</code> when any mark, a row's checkbox included, was pressed on what a
                <code>Source</code> since replaced showed. For a row command waiting behind earlier input, <code>RowLeftTheWindow</code> means
                its identity left the Window while waiting. The grid announces that queued refusal;
                the Example below also writes the Consumer’s own words. A press on a row's checkbox whose row a live update re-rendered before the click arrived is
                still heard: the grid answers it itself, marking the row or saying why it cannot.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>GridColumn.TemplateColumn(...)</code></strong> paints its cells with your markup. It still
                takes a value accessor, and that value — never the markup — is what sorts, filters and copies.
                Keep the template in a static field: a new lambda per render makes the column look changed.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>A template's control can take the keyboard.</strong> <kbd>Space</kbd> on the cell asks for it:
                the context's <code>FocusRequest</code> turns non-zero on one render. The control answers by handing its
                element to the grid's <code>HandKeyboardToAsync</code>, not with its own <code>FocusAsync</code>. Over
                Blazor Server the request lands a round trip after the <kbd>Space</kbd>, and the grid gives the keyboard
                only while it is still the grid's, so a user who has moved on to another grid in the meantime keeps it
                there. The Example's Note column does this, with the grid cascaded to its control; <kbd>Esc</kbd> brings
                the keyboard back. What is typed there is the page's, kept by position, and the column's value reads it
                back, so a note sorts, filters and copies like any other value.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong><code>GridColumn.ActionColumn(...)</code></strong> carries buttons and no value. A press raises
                <code>OnAction</code> with the row and the action's name. With the keyboard, Space presses a single
                action, or enters a cell with several, where ← and → choose and Space presses the one chosen.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Under live data</strong>, a press acts on the row it was made on, as that row is now: a value
                the feed changed since the button was painted refuses nothing, and whether the action is still
                allowed is your rule in <code>OnAction</code>. With a Row Key, a press finds its row wherever the order
                has moved it. A press the grid can no longer pair with its row is refused through
                <code>OnActionRefused</code>, naming why: <code>OrderMoved</code>, <code>RowLeftTheWindow</code>, or
                <code>SourceChanged</code> when the <code>Source</code> was replaced. See
                <a href="exgrid/editing#editing-while-values-change">Editing while values change</a>.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Row Action and row Mark clicks wait for earlier input, including an asynchronous Consumer
            callback. They follow the current row by Row Key, or by instance without one, only while
            it stays in the Window. Leaving the painted slice by scrolling is harmless. Leaving the
            Window invalidates the waiting command permanently, even if the row returns before its
            turn: it is refused as <code>RowLeftTheWindow</code>, and later inputs continue. Replacing
            the Source instead reports <code>SourceChanged</code>. The grid announces the refusal;
            <code>KeyRefusalLabelIds.RowCommandTargetLeft</code> supplies customizable words.
        </p>
        <p>
            When no earlier input is held in the browser, successive row Marks are sent immediately
            in order. Marking several rows before using a page button does not add a round trip
            between Marks. A Mark behind a held key or Action still waits for that earlier input.
            On Blazor Server, a row click made while the connection is down is refused when the
            next input after reconnection exposes the loss; later input can continue.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Row commands behind an asynchronous Consumer" Builtin="typeof(QueuedRowCommands)"
                     Mud="typeof(QueuedRowCommandsMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Grid/QueuedRowCommands.razor"])">
            Focus the grid and press <kbd>Control+B</kbd> (Command+B on macOS), then click Open on row 0
            and the mark on row 1. Remove the first two rows, restore them, then Release: both commands
            are refused. Click again to see new commands succeed. Without removing rows, Release lets
            the waiting commands run in the order you made them.
        </DocsExample>
        <p>
            In ExGrid for React the three are <code>markColumn()</code> and <code>actionColumn(name, actions)</code>
            from <code>@@ex-grid/core</code>, and <code>templateColumn(column, render)</code> from
            <code>@@ex-grid/react</code>, whose <code>render</code> returns React elements; <code>GridSource.from</code>
            keeps the marks as <code>source.marks</code>, a refused mark reaches <code>onRefused</code> with kind
            <code>'mark'</code>, and a press raises <code>onAction</code>. A template's control takes the keyboard by
            handing its element to the cell's <code>takeKeyboard</code> from a layout effect when
            <code>focusRequest</code> turns non-zero.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Marks, a template and actions" Builtin="typeof(SpecialColumns)" Mud="typeof(SpecialColumnsMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/NoteField.razor", "Examples/Grid/Position.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Columns built at run time">
        <p>
            When the data's columns are not known until the program runs, as when an AI has just fetched it or a saved
            workflow binds whatever its last step produced, there is no row type to write columns for.
            <code>ShapedGrid</code> builds them from the data's shape instead: one column per column a grid's spec shows,
            over a Snapshot, each row a <code>ShapedRow</code>, a position in it. Hand its <code>Columns</code>,
            <code>Source</code> and <code>PinnedColumnCount</code> to an <code>ExGrid</code> of <code>TRow="ShapedRow"</code>.
            The sort and the filter are executed by the source with <code>GridSource.From</code>'s semantics.
        </p>
        <p>
            The spec is JSON. It names the input it shows in <code>data</code> and its <code>columns</code> in order, each
            with an optional <code>header</code> and <code>format</code>; a column's <code>width</code> and
            <code>pinned</code>, the <code>sorts</code> and the <code>filter</code> are the View State's JSON, read by the same
            reader. <code>GridViewSpec.Read</code> takes each input's shape, its columns' names and kinds, and checks the
            spec against it before any row is drawn.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>A decimal or double column must carry its format</strong>, an Excel format code in the invariant
            spelling: <code>#,##0.00</code>, <code>0.00%</code>, <code>0.00000</code>. One left out is refused, naming the
            column, because the places depend on what the column holds — yen, basis points or an FX rate — and a default
            guessed from the type would show a rate of <code>1.08734</code> as <code>1.09</code>. An integer shows as
            <code>#,##0</code> and a date as <code>yyyy-mm-dd</code> unless the spec writes otherwise (a date holding a
            time of day as <code>yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm:ss</code>, so the time is never hidden); text and booleans show as
            they are. A code may have up to three sections (positive; negative; zero), quoted text, <code>%</code> and
            exponents; colours and conditions in brackets, <code>@@</code>, <code>_</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>?</code>
            and fractions are refused rather than shown some other way. A format that is merely wrong, two places on a
            rate, is not caught: it is written in the spec, where a person reading it sees it.
        </p>
        <p>
            The data itself may arrive as JSON, which tells no decimal from a double and has no dates. A
            <code>BoardInput</code> declares each column's kind, and <code>FromJson</code> converts by the declaration;
            a value that does not convert is refused by its row and column, never dropped or read as zero. Edit the
            texts below and draw again: remove the rate's format, or write <code>"abc"</code> as a notional.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="A grid from JSON, its columns built at run time" Builtin="typeof(ShapedColumns)" Mud="typeof(ShapedColumnsMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Grid/ShapedTrades.cs"])" />
        <p>
            <strong>Rows held on a server</strong> are fetched a page at a time instead: <code>ShapedGrid.Fetch</code> builds
            the same columns from the spec alone, and its source hands each fetch the grid's query, its range, sort and
            filter, to be answered with a <code>ShapedPage</code>, the rows as a Snapshot (an Arrow stream read with
            <code>SnapshotArrow</code>, say), where they start and how many the result holds. Each page is checked against
            the spec as a whole Snapshot is: one lacking a column, or holding it of another kind, fails its fetch, naming
            the input, and is never shown. A shape cannot say whether a later page will hold a time of day, so a date column
            with no format shows <code>yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm:ss</code> there; write <code>yyyy-mm-dd</code> to show dates only.
            When the fetch reads state of your own that changes, <code>FetchingGridSource.Requery</code> asks again from
            the top and drops the selection, as a new sort does. ExBoard's
            <a href="exboard">Board over a server</a> draws one.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

